- First, put both models onto the same scale using a simple calibration step on their OOF predictions:
  - For each model separately, fit a one-dimensional monotonic map from OOF preds to target using only training data.
  - The easiest safe version is piecewise rank calibration: convert OOF preds to ranks, map those ranks to the empirical target quantiles, and apply the same mapping to test preds.
- After calibration, blend the two calibrated predictions with a geometric mean in log-space after shifting by a constant if needed, or more simply use median-of-two style blending:
  - final_pred = median(pred_A_cal, pred_B_cal)
  - This is more robust than weighted averaging when one model has occasional large errors.
- If you want a slightly stronger but still easy variant, do a fold-wise vote:
  - Split the training data once with the same KFold used by Model B.
  - On each fold, calibrate both models on the fold’s OOF values, then take the fold-wise median blend.
  - Average the fold-wise blended test predictions across folds.
- Use a small residual correction only if needed:
  - Fit a one-feature linear model on the blended OOF predictions versus y to remove bias.
  - Apply the same linear correction to the test blend.
- This plan stays close to the original code, avoids stacking complexity, and is different from weighted averaging or standard meta-model stacking.
